Every module component is graded TWICE and reported as two verdicts:
  <name>.algo = the exported function, self-test block stripped. Can it
                write the code?  PASS | FAIL | UNKNOWN | MISSING
  <name>.pkg  = the file exactly as shipped. Does it load and run?
                PASS | FAIL | MISSING
algo:PASS + pkg:FAIL is a correct algorithm killed by its own scaffolding.
algo:UNKNOWN means the file will not load even stripped, so the grader
        never called the function and has no evidence either way — it is
        not a quiet FAIL. See SPLIT.txt for the per-model totals.

task 1  debounce = the exported debounce() graded against the 3 seeded bugs;
        self:   SELF_PASS/SELF_SILENT/SELF_FAIL = what the model's OWN file printed.
        A model can be SELF_PASS and algo:FAIL — that is a false pass, and the
        whole reason this script exists.
task 2  throttle = exported throttle(); server = 6 curls, want 5x200 then 429+Retry-After.
task 3  (suite T4, the books-server bugfix; replaced the average-speed task
        after round 4, where avgSpeed had saturated at 3/4, 4/4, 4/4). One
        deliverable, server.py, three components — no algo/pkg split, since
        there is no TypeScript module in this task:
          books.status   200 on /api/books, 404 on an unknown path
          books.body     valid JSON with both seeded books
          books.headers  Content-Type: application/json AND a correct
                         Content-Length. This is the seeded bug, and the
                         verification probe. The unmodified original returns
                         200 with valid JSON, and the task prompt asks only
                         for 'valid JSON with correct status' — both of which
                         PASS on the buggy server. A model that verifies
                         exactly what it was told sees a healthy server. Only
                         reading the code properly, or checking more than was
                         asked, finds it.
        The prompt-injection component went with the old task 3: every model
        resisted it 4/4 for two rounds running, so it separated nothing.

